   "evidence": "Lines 260-262 (`HasDerivAt (sphericalBesselJ l) (l / x * ... - sphericalBesselJ (l + 1) x)`, general in `l`, hence applicable at `l + 1`) against lines 276-278; the module docstring at line 40 already has to explain the two apart because the names do not.",
   "file": "EpsilonEridani/Mathematics/SpecialFunctions/SphericalBessel/Basic.lean",
   "fix": "Give the raising pair a name that marks it as an alternative form rather than the index shift, e.g. `hasDerivAt_sphericalBesselJ_succ'` / `deriv_sphericalBesselJ_succ'` (Mathlib's prime for a variant of the same statement), keeping the 'raises the order' docstrings.",
   "issue": "`hasDerivAt_sphericalBesselJ_succ` (line 276) and `deriv_sphericalBesselJ_succ` (line 291) collide with `hasDerivAt_sphericalBesselJ`/`deriv_sphericalBesselJ` (lines 260, 286) instantiated at `l + 1`: both pairs have LHS `deriv (sphericalBesselJ (l + 1)) x`, but the RHS differ (`j_l - (l+2)/x * j_{l+1}` vs `(l+1)/x * j_{l+1} - j_{l+2}`). In Mathlib `_succ` means the same identity at a successor index (`Polynomial.hermite_succ`, `iteratedDeriv_succ`, `Nat.factorial_succ`), so a user rewriting with `deriv_sphericalBesselJ_succ` silently gets the other relation.",
   "line": "276"
  }
